-- Passage rg10-figurative-language-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('55e04f37-dcb7-5914-9a5b-d7401c418c67', 'rg10-figurative-language-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$The Piano in the Driveway$q$, $q$The piano arrived on a Saturday, strapped to a borrowed trailer and wrapped in two moving blankets. Mrs. Halvorsen, who had owned it for forty years, had described it on the phone as "a piano with a lot of history." Luis Echeverria and his father soon learned what that meant.

One leg wobbled. Three of the white keys had lost their ivory tops, and the lowest notes buzzed as if a bee were trapped inside. When Luis pressed middle C, the sound arrived a moment late, like a guest who had stopped to take off his coat.

His father ran a hand along the scratched lid. "Mrs. Halvorsen called it vintage," he said. "I would have called it old."

"Isn't that the same thing?" Luis asked.

"Not to the person selling it," his father said, smiling.

They spent the afternoon working in the driveway. His father tightened the leg with a new bolt. Luis found a matching set of key tops online and glued them on with a steady hand. A neighbor who tuned pianos on weekends stopped by and spent an hour adjusting the strings, humming each note under her breath until it matched her tuning fork.

By evening, the buzz was gone and middle C answered right away. Luis played the only song he knew by heart, slowly at first and then with more confidence. Two kids from across the street wandered over and sat on the curb to listen.

When the song ended, his father nodded at the instrument. "Now," he said, "it is not old and it is not vintage. It is ours." Luis decided that was the best word of all.$q$, 'draft')
