values
  ('3667ac3c-23fb-57dc-a249-9e57570c308d', null, (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null, 'hard',
   $q$Amara "did not want to hide the truth, but she wanted visitors to see the same dog she had come to know." How does this sentence explain the difference between her new card and the shelter's old euphemisms?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Her card replaces every description with the single word \"friendly.\"","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"Her card uses more euphemisms in order to trick families into adopting Biscuit.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misreads-tone"},{"text":"Her card lists only Biscuit's problems so that families are fully warned.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-text"},{"text":"Her words keep the facts but choose connotations that match his real nature.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Her words keep the facts but choose connotations that match his real nature.$q$, $q$She keeps what is true about Biscuit (he is loud and strong-willed) but picks words like "talkative" that reflect the affectionate dog she knows, unlike vague labels that just cover problems. Her goal was not to "hide the truth," so tricking families does not fit.$q$, 'draft', '2e36ce9a-2dca-5cff-a90b-752aeb4ce2be')
