-- Passage rg10-figurative-language-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('a64d80fc-e58f-572f-aac4-f572782d3a6c', 'rg10-figurative-language-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g10-figurative-language'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Selling Winter$q$, $q$In the early 1800s, a young merchant in New England proposed what his neighbors considered a ridiculous plan. He would cut blocks of ice from frozen ponds in winter, pack them into ships and sell them in hot ports thousands of miles away. Many people called the idea a gamble; a few, more kindly, called it a venture.

The first voyages were disappointing. Much of the cargo melted before the ships arrived, and buyers in warm cities had little idea what to do with ice. The merchant found himself in a strange position: he was rich in a product that was literally disappearing beneath his feet.

Instead of giving up, he studied the problem. Workers learned to cut the ice into uniform blocks that could be stacked tightly, leaving fewer gaps for warm air. Sawdust, a waste product from lumber mills, turned out to be an excellent insulator. Ice houses with thick double walls kept shipments frozen for months. Slowly, the melting losses shrank.

The merchant also had to create demand. He offered cold drinks to restaurant owners for free, betting that once customers tasted them, they would refuse to go back. The strategy worked. Within a few decades, ice from northern ponds was being shipped across oceans, and people in distant cities had come to treat it as a necessity rather than a luxury.

The trade lasted until mechanical refrigeration made natural ice unnecessary. Today, the story is remembered less for the ice itself than for the stubborn imagination behind it. The merchant's neighbors had seen frozen ponds as a nuisance. He had seen them as a harvest waiting for the right market.$q$, 'draft')
